#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Bounded native series-elastic-coordinate probe for the DRG80 screw.

This is a component preflight, not fixture qualification.  It tests one frozen
constitutive question under known-pair bilateral contact: a passive, fixed-zero
native prismatic force drive (K=1e6 N/m, D=4000 N*s/m) is placed in series with
the already-observed hard 1 mm/revolution helical constraint, so the physical
output coordinate is z=e+p*theta.  The drive target is never changed and no
pose, velocity, joint state, or contact state is written after reset.

The two auxiliary coordinate links receive only 1 mg each and have no
collision geometry; the physical screw rotor receives the remaining normative
socket-adjuster mass, principal inertia, and known-pair collision geometry.
This avoids the rejected proposal's hidden tenfold rotor inertia and 10 g
support while keeping the auxiliary coordinates explicit.  The complete
common-frame and reduced-mass comparisons below quantify this disclosed
representation approximation rather than calling it exact preservation.
At every exercised state the probe transforms every moving link's native COM
and inertia into one world frame, forms the aggregate spatial properties, and
compares them with the intended single moving part.  It also reduces the full
native 3x3 generalized mass matrix with the frozen coordinate map
T=[[1,0],[0,p],[0,1]] (in [e,h,theta] order), reports both reduced matrices,
and gates every mixed-unit entry on its own physical scale.

The implicit drive force is never reconstructed from its authored K/D values.
It is inferred at every fresh POST_PHYSICS_STEP sample from delta(M v) after
subtracting measured known-pair contact impulse and commanded generalized
impulse.  Effective K and D, and spring work, are fitted from that independent
native inference.
"""

